{
  "id": "determine-if-two-strings-are-close",
  "questionId": "determine-if-two-strings-are-close",
  "slug": "determine-if-two-strings-are-close",
  "title": "Determine if Two Strings Are Close",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Hash map",
  "patterns": [
    "hash map",
    "frequency counting"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "swapping characters means positions don't matter at all - only how many of each letter you have. what does 'replace every X with Y' do to those counts?",
    "the replace operation lets you relabel which letter owns which count, but it can't change the SET of counts themselves, and it can't invent a new letter that wasn't already present",
    "two conditions must both hold: the two strings must use exactly the same set of distinct letters, and the sorted list of frequency counts must match exactly",
    "build a frequency map for each string, check the key sets are equal, then check the sorted lists of values are equal"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "checking only the frequency counts and forgetting to also check that the same set of letters is used",
    "not sorting the counts before comparing them (two strings can have the same counts assigned to different letters)",
    "forgetting the length check, which the frequency-set comparison actually already implies but is easy to special-case incorrectly"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Two strings are considered \"close\" if you can make them identical\nusing any number of these two operations, in any order: swap the\npositions of any two existing characters, or replace every occurrence\nof one existing character with another existing character (and vice\nversa).\n\n**Determine whether `word1` and `word2` are close.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= word1.length, word2.length <= 100",
      "word1 and word2 consist only of lowercase English letters",
      "operations may be applied any number of times, in any order"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "different lengths (always false)",
      "the two strings use a different SET of letters, even if counts would otherwise match",
      "identical strings"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "word1 = \"abc\", word2 = \"bca\"",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "swapping characters can turn abc into bca directly, since they use the same letters"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "word1 = \"a\", word2 = \"aa\"",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "different lengths can never be made equal"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "word1 = \"cabbba\", word2 = \"abbccc\"",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "both use the letters a, b, c with counts that can be matched up: {1,3,2} and {1,3,2}"
      }
